{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342205,"nonce":"4Tql36Is6R2Mh76gfO13r6VHSx8=","title":"Tiered execution keeps up to four HIR copies of each function for the whole run","message":"install_lazy_compiler keeps: the lowered module (Arc\u003cHirModule\u003e, held by current_module, loaded, every FunctionEntry and the LLVM module_context); a scratch module that Scratch::of makes as a full clone of that module at the first lazy optimisation and clears only after 250 ms of worker idleness, which a short run never reaches; interp_bodies (make_interp_body's clone plus the release pass, never removed, including run-once chunk segments); and optimized_bodies (every body with a loop even on the Cranelift-only ladder, every body on an LLVM ladder). OSR outlined regions and bytecode come on top.\n\nEvidence. NUC instrumented, nextvar default tier at exit: module 110.5 MB + scratch 111.3 (830 functions) + interp_bodies 78.0 (49 bodies; lua$chunk$3 alone 32.3) + optimized_bodies 55.8 (83 bodies) = about 355 MB of HIR, heap 423 MB; with the capacity fix 53.6 + 46.8 + 23.2 + 19.9. files: the scratch clone measured 506 MB. Mac, MallocStackLogging at _exit, locals.lua zt (807 MB live malloc, 4.2 MB GC slabs, fails at line 315 having run little): lowered HIR 330, interp bodies 141, optimised bodies 134, Cranelift code 50, OSR outlines 19, bytecode 16; locals zl adds scratch 187. coroutine zt: scratch 118, lowered 106. gc.lua zt: scratch 73, lowered 62. Benchmarks: the scratch is 8.5 to 15.4 MB of every run (nbody 15.4, mandelbrot 11.7, binary_trees 11.3, tokenize 11.1), optimised bodies 3 to 9 MB.\n\nSavings: nextvar 190 to 245 MB beyond the module (about 90 after the capacity fix); locals about 300 to 460; coroutine about 120; gc.lua about 75; each benchmark 8 to 20 MB.\n\nFix: the scratch holds only what optimising a body reads (the body and its inline candidates, as Arc\u003cHirFunction\u003e entries copied on write) and is released when the lazy queue empties; an interp body is dropped once its function has native code and no interpreted frame of it is live; an optimised body is dropped once its function is at the top of its ladder and no frame can ask for resume points; a re-request rebuilds rather than fails. Builds on the optimised-body cells of fix/llvm-no-reopt (73e6e26). Package MEM-tier-body-retention.","files":null}]}